{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Welcome to the Arrays course. Before any code, let's answer the most important question: **why do arrays exist at all?** Once you see the problem they solve, everything else in this course will make sense."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A small problem that gets big fast"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Imagine you're writing a program for a teacher. There are five students, and you need to store their marks and print the class average. With plain variables, it looks like this:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "let mark1 = 72;\nlet mark2 = 85;\nlet mark3 = 64;\nlet mark4 = 91;\nlet mark5 = 58;\n\nconst average = (mark1 + mark2 + mark3 + mark4 + mark5) / 5;"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That works for five students. Now the school has **500**. You'd need 500 variables, a 500-term sum, and a new line of code every time a student joins. You can't even write a loop, because there's no way to say \"the next variable\" — `mark1` and `mark2` are just unrelated names to the computer."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/d124cb25-dd90-4cb9-a348-55cbee0abeb1.svg",
      "alt": "On the left, five separate variables mark1 to mark5 scattered around, each with its own name. On the right, one array called marks holding the same five values side by side, numbered 0 to 4.",
      "width": 832,
      "height": 599,
