    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**One name for many values.** `marks` holds the whole class. You pass one thing around, not 500.",
        "**Instant access by position.** Reading `marks[437]` takes the same tiny amount of time as reading `marks[0]` — the computer calculates where slot 437 is instead of searching for it. You'll see exactly how in the next lesson; it's written **O(1)**, \"constant time\".",
        "**Loops work naturally.** Because indexes are just numbers 0, 1, 2, …, a loop variable can visit every slot. Summing, searching, sorting and printing all become a few lines."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "…and the price you pay"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Arrays aren't magic. Their strength — values packed side by side in fixed slots — also creates their weaknesses:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Inserting or deleting in the middle is slow.** To make room at index 2, every value after it must shift one place right. For a big array that's a lot of moving.",
        "**Finding a value (not a position) means looking.** \"Where is 91?\" has no shortcut in an unsorted array: you check slots one by one.",
        "**The size is fixed** in languages like Java and C, because the slots were reserved up front. \"Growable\" arrays (JavaScript arrays, Python lists, Java's `ArrayList`, C++'s `vector`) hide this by quietly moving everything to a bigger block when they fill up — you'll build one yourself at the end of this course."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Much of this course is about getting around these weaknesses: clever orders of visiting, extra helper arrays, sorting first, or pairing an array with a hash map."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Where you'll meet arrays"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Almost everywhere. A **string** is an array of characters. An **image** is a 2D array of pixels. A spreadsheet, a music playlist, a leaderboard, the frames of a video — all arrays. Even other data structures — stacks, queues, heaps and hash tables — are usually built on top of an array. Learning arrays well pays off in every course that follows."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What's next"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The rest of this chapter opens the box: how an array actually sits in your computer's memory, why that makes `marks[i]` so fast, and why inserting in the middle is slow. Then **Using arrays in code** shows the everyday syntax in four languages, and **Solving problems with arrays** teaches you when to reach for one."
    }
  ],
